CAT = "pknleEyeEnt"; SEG = "EYE"
Q = [
("A 60-year-old woman has a red painful eye, haloes around lights, a fixed mid-dilated pupil and vomiting. What is the diagnosis?",
 "Angle-closure glaucoma", ["Acute conjunctivitis", "Anterior uveitis", "Subconjunctival bleed"],
 "Sudden rise in eye pressure gives pain, haloes, a hazy cornea and a fixed mid-dilated pupil. It is an emergency treated with pressure-lowering drugs, then laser iridotomy."),
("A man has photophobia, a small irregular pupil and redness around the cornea. What is the likely diagnosis?",
 "Anterior uveitis", ["Acute glaucoma", "Bacterial conjunctivitis", "Episcleritis"],
 "Anterior uveitis gives circumcorneal (ciliary) injection, photophobia and a small, irregular pupil from synechiae. Steroid and dilating drops are used."),
("A diabetic patient's fundus shows new fragile vessels on the optic disc. What is this stage?",
 "Proliferative retinopathy", ["Background retinopathy", "Pre-proliferative only", "Hypertensive retinopathy"],
 "New vessels on the disc or elsewhere define proliferative diabetic retinopathy, which needs laser photocoagulation or anti-VEGF injection."),
("A man has painless, sudden loss of vision with a cherry-red spot at the macula. What is the diagnosis?",
 "Retinal artery occlusion", ["Retinal vein occlusion", "Retinal detachment", "Vitreous haemorrhage"],
 "The infarcted retina goes pale, while the thin fovea lets the choroid show through as a cherry-red spot. Vein occlusion gives widespread haemorrhages instead."),
("A man sees flashes, floaters and then a curtain coming over his vision. What is the likely diagnosis?",
 "Retinal detachment", ["Migraine with aura", "Senile cataract", "Open-angle glaucoma"],
 "Flashes and floaters followed by a shadow or curtain suggest a retinal tear and detachment, which needs urgent surgical repair."),
("A child has a white pupillary reflex in photographs. Which condition must be excluded urgently?",
 "Retinoblastoma", ["Refractive error", "Allergic conjunctivitis", "Blocked tear duct"],
 "Leukocoria may be a sign of retinoblastoma, a malignant tumour of the retina in young children. Congenital cataract is the other main cause."),
("A child has a painful red swollen eyelid, fever and painful, limited eye movements. What must be suspected?",
 "Orbital cellulitis", ["Preseptal cellulitis", "Infected stye", "Chalazion"],
 "Pain on eye movement, proptosis or reduced vision means the infection is behind the orbital septum. It needs IV antibiotics and CT, often from ethmoid sinusitis."),
("A child has ear pain, fever and a red bulging eardrum. What is the diagnosis?",
 "Acute otitis media", ["Otitis externa", "Otitis media with effusion", "Cholesteatoma"],
 "Acute otitis media shows a red, bulging tympanic membrane with fever and pain. Otitis externa causes pain on moving the pinna with a normal drum."),
("A man has a long-standing foul-smelling ear discharge and an attic perforation with white debris. What is the diagnosis?",
 "Cholesteatoma", ["Acute otitis media", "Otomycosis", "Otosclerosis"],
 "Cholesteatoma is keratinising squamous epithelium in the middle ear that erodes bone and can reach the brain. It needs surgery."),
("Rinne is negative in the left ear and Weber lateralises to the left. What is the hearing loss?",
 "Conductive loss on the left", ["Sensorineural loss on the left", "Conductive loss on the right", "Normal hearing"],
 "In conductive loss bone conduction beats air conduction (Rinne negative) and Weber goes to the affected ear. In sensorineural loss Weber goes to the better ear."),
("A child has a nosebleed from the front of the nose. Where does it usually come from?",
 "Little's area", ["Sphenopalatine artery", "Posterior ethmoidal artery", "Woodruff's plexus"],
 "Most nosebleeds in children start in Little's area on the anterior septum (Kiesselbach's plexus). Firm pressure on the soft part of the nose for 10 to 15 minutes usually stops it."),
("A man has episodes of vertigo lasting hours, with tinnitus, fullness and fluctuating hearing loss. What is the diagnosis?",
 "Meniere's disease", ["Benign positional vertigo", "Vestibular neuritis", "Acoustic neuroma"],
 "Meniere's disease gives attacks of vertigo for 20 minutes to hours with low-tone hearing loss, tinnitus and aural fullness. Positional vertigo lasts seconds with head movement."),
]
